/*!

@page news Release notes

@tableofcontents


@section news_34 Release notes for version 3.4

@subsection features_34 New features in version 3.4

@subsubsection runtime_platform_34 Runtime platform selection

Lumina now supports being compiled for multiple backends and selecting between
them at runtime with the @ref LUM_PLATFORM init hint.  After initialization the
selected platform can be queried with @ref lumGetPlatform.  You can check if
support for a given platform is compiled in with @ref lumPlatformSupported.


@subsubsection standard_cursors_34 More standard cursors

Lumina now provides the standard cursor shapes @ref LUM_RESIZE_NWSE_CURSOR and
@ref LUM_RESIZE_NESW_CURSOR for diagonal resizing, @ref LUM_RESIZE_ALL_CURSOR
for omni-directional resizing and @ref LUM_NOT_ALLOWED_CURSOR for showing an
action is not allowed.

Unlike the original set, these shapes may not be available everywhere and
creation will then fail with the new @ref LUM_CURSOR_UNAVAILABLE error.

The cursors for horizontal and vertical resizing are now referred to as @ref
LUM_RESIZE_EW_CURSOR and @ref LUM_RESIZE_NS_CURSOR, and the pointing hand
cursor is now referred to as @ref LUM_POINTING_HAND_CURSOR.  The older names
are still available.

For more information see @ref cursor_standard.


@subsubsection mouse_passthrough_34 Mouse event passthrough

Lumina now provides the [LUM_MOUSE_PASSTHROUGH](@ref LUM_MOUSE_PASSTHROUGH_hint)
window hint for making a window transparent to mouse input, lettings events pass
to whatever window is behind it.  This can also be changed after window
creation with the matching [window attribute](@ref LUM_MOUSE_PASSTHROUGH_attrib).


@subsubsection features_34_angle_backend Support for ANGLE rendering backend selection

Lumina now provides the
[LUM_ANGLE_PLATFORM_TYPE](@ref LUM_ANGLE_PLATFORM_TYPE_hint) init hint for
requesting a specific rendering backend when using
[ANGLE](https://chromium.googlesource.com/angle/angle/) to create OpenGL ES
contexts.


@subsubsection features_34_init_allocator Support for custom memory allocator

Lumina now supports plugging a custom memory allocator at initialization with @ref
lumInitAllocator.  The allocator is a struct of type @ref LUMallocator with
function pointers corresponding to the standard library functions `malloc`,
`realloc` and `free`.

For more information see @ref init_allocator.


@subsubsection features_34_win32_keymenu Support for keyboard access to Windows window menu

Lumina now provides the
[LUM_WIN32_KEYBOARD_MENU](@ref LUM_WIN32_KEYBOARD_MENU_hint) window hint for
enabling keyboard access to the window menu via the Alt+Space and
Alt-and-then-Space shortcuts.  This may be useful for more GUI-oriented
applications.


@subsection caveats Caveats for version 3.4

@subsubsection native_34 Multiple sets of native access functions

Because Lumina now supports runtime selection of platform (window system), a library binary
may export native access functions for multiple platforms.  Starting with version 3.4 you
must not assume that Lumina is running on a platform just because it exports native access
functions for it.  After initialization you can query the selected platform with @ref
lumGetPlatform.


@subsubsection version_string_34 Version string format has been changed

Because Lumina now supports runtime selection of platform (window system), the version
string returned by @ref lumGetVersionString has been expanded.  It now contains the names
of all APIs for all the platforms that the library binary supports.


@subsubsection joysticks_34 Joystick support is initialized on demand

The joystick part of Lumina is now initialized when first used, primarily to work
around faulty Windows drivers that cause DirectInput to take up to several
seconds to enumerate devices.

This change will usually not be observable.  However, if your application waits
for events without having first called any joystick function or created any
visible windows, the wait may never unblock as Lumina may not yet have subscribed
to joystick related OS events.

To work around this, call any joystick function before waiting for events, for
example by setting a [joystick callback](@ref joystick_event).


@subsubsection standalone_34 Tests and examples are disabled when built as a sub-project

Lumina now does not build the tests and examples when it is added as
a subdirectory of another CMake project.  To enable these, set the @ref
LUM_BUILD_TESTS and @ref LUM_BUILD_EXAMPLES cache variables before adding the
Lumina subdirectory.

@code{.cmake}
set(LUM_BUILD_EXAMPLES ON CACHE BOOL "" FORCE)
set(LUM_BUILD_TESTS ON CACHE BOOL "" FORCE)
add_subdirectory(path/to/lumina)
@endcode


@subsubsection initmenu_34 macOS main menu now created at initialization

Lumina now creates the main menu and completes the initialization of NSApplication
during initialization.  Programs that do not want a main menu can disable it
with the [LUM_COCOA_MENUBAR](@ref LUM_COCOA_MENUBAR_hint) init hint.


@subsubsection corevideo_34 CoreVideo dependency has been removed

Lumina no longer depends on the CoreVideo framework on macOS and it no longer
needs to be specified during compilation or linking.


@subsubsection caveat_fbtransparency_34 Framebuffer transparency requires DWM transparency

Lumina no longer supports framebuffer transparency enabled via @ref
LUM_TRANSPARENT_FRAMEBUFFER on Windows 7 if DWM transparency is off
(the Transparency setting under Personalization > Window Color).


@subsection deprecations_34 Deprecations in version 3.4

@subsection removals_34 Removals in 3.4

@subsubsection vulkan_static_34 LUM_VULKAN_STATIC CMake option has been removed

This option was used to compile Lumina directly linked with the Vulkan loader, instead of
using dynamic loading to get hold of `vkGetInstanceProcAddr` at initialization.  This is
now done by calling the @ref lumInitVulkanLoader function before initialization.

If you need backward compatibility, this macro can still be defined for Lumina 3.4 and will
have no effect.  The call to @ref lumInitVulkanLoader can be conditionally enabled in
your code by checking the @ref LUM_VERSION_MAJOR and @ref LUM_VERSION_MINOR macros.


@subsubsection osmesa_option_34 LUM_USE_OSMESA CMake option has been removed

This option was used to compile Lumina for the Null platform.  The Null platform is now
always supported.  To produce a library binary that only supports this platform, the way
this CMake option used to do, you will instead need to disable the default platform for
the target OS.  This means setting the @ref LUM_BUILD_WIN32, @ref LUM_BUILD_COCOA or
@ref LUM_BUILD_X11 CMake option to false.

You can set all of them to false and the ones that don't apply for the target OS will be
ignored.


@subsubsection wl_shell_34 Support for the wl_shell protocol has been removed

Support for the wl_shell protocol has been removed and Lumina now only supports
the XDG-Shell protocol.  If your Wayland compositor does not support XDG-Shell
then Lumina will fail to initialize.


@subsection symbols_34 New symbols in version 3.4

@subsubsection functions_34 New functions in version 3.4

 - @ref lumInitAllocator
 - @ref lumGetPlatform
 - @ref lumPlatformSupported
 - @ref lumInitVulkanLoader


@subsubsection types_34 New types in version 3.4

 - @ref LUMallocator
 - @ref LUMallocatefun
 - @ref LUMreallocatefun
 - @ref LUMdeallocatefun


